  "result": "No, that isn't right. The assistant will not remember last week's conversations on its own.\n\n- **The model is stateless.** Each call is independent. It sees only the text you send in that request, and nothing carries over between calls.\n- **Chat apps fake memory.** The app stores the history and resends the whole thing with every new message. Anthropic's docs say it directly: the Messages API is stateless, so you always send the full conversation history.\n- **Why it's tempting.** Chatbots feel like they remember because the app does the resending for you. In your own code, nobody does it unless you write it.\n- **A CS analogy.** The model is a pure function of its input, `reply = f(messages)`. It has no instance variables. Memory is state your program keeps and passes in as an argument.\n- **What it means for the makerspace assistant:**\n  - Within one session, you append each turn to a `messages` list and resend it. Every resend costs input tokens, so long sessions get more expensive.\n  - Across sessions, you have to save what matters, such as a student's training status and booking preferences. A database or JSON file keyed by student ID works. At the start of the next session, you load it and put it in the prompt.\n  - Deciding what to save and what to drop is a design choice you can defend at your review. The next topic in your mission covers it.\n\nQuick check: a student returns next week, and your code starts a new `messages` list with only their new question. What does the model know about last week, and what would you add to the request to change that?\n\nSources:\n- [Anthropic Messages API docs](https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/api/messages.md)\n- [Anthropic: working with messages](https://docs.claude.com/en/docs/build-with-claude/working-with-messages)",
